from pathlib import Path
sections=[]
def section(title,intro=''):
 s={'title':title,'intro':intro,'blocks':[]};sections.append(s);return s

def para(s,text):s['blocks'].append(('p',text))
def heading(s,text):s['blocks'].append(('h2',text))
def req(s,id,status,text):s['blocks'].append(('req',id,status,text))
def table(s,headers,rows,widths=None):s['blocks'].append(('table',headers,rows,widths))
def figure(s,file,caption):s['blocks'].append(('figure',file,caption))

s=section('1 Product intent and document status','DevWorks is a desktop application that combines project planning, code intelligence, source control visibility, model routing, and an autonomous coding harness. Its main interface is a conversation with a workspace coordinator. The user can oversee all work, inspect any execution, and redirect it without operating a separate application backend.')
para(s,'This document consolidates the conversation and the reviewed UI prototype through the approved revision 08 UI refresh, including Current / Chat History, floating completion notifications and integrated page headers. It is a requirements review draft, not an implementation plan or a claim that every design detail has been approved. Further UI and behavior changes are expected before build work begins.')
heading(s,'How to read requirement status')
table(s,['Status','Meaning'],[['Confirmed','An explicit user requirement or a direction accepted during this discussion. Confidence is high in the intent; detailed implementation may remain open.'],['Proposed','An architectural or behavioral detail added to make the requirements coherent and testable. Confidence is medium until reviewed or demonstrated.'],['Open','A decision that has not been made. These items must remain visible rather than becoming implicit implementation choices.']],[1.0,5.9])
para(s,'Requirement IDs are stable review references. Confirmed and proposed requirements together describe the intended capability surface of this draft; a proposed item is not a silently authorized commitment. Open decisions are listed in Section 18. Exact toolkit versions, schemas, APIs, budgets, and performance thresholds are not frozen by this document.')
heading(s,'Scope and sources')
para(s,'The existing CodeGraph, dark-factory, and simple-router repositories are sources of behavior and lessons. The target is a rewrite in one primary language, not a desktop shell around the current .NET services. CodeGraph supplies many core capabilities, but a complete port of every CodeGraph endpoint or administrative feature has not been requested. A capability review must settle the remaining boundary before implementation is represented as complete.')
para(s,'CodeGraph feature requirements and items are the operational source of truth for approved requirements and delivery. Versioned authoring sources, generated documents and the UI reference publish the same decisions for review. Shortcut is not used for this project. Requirement approval and prototype validation do not establish native implementation or acceptance; preserve stable IDs and decision provenance when synchronizing changes.')

s=section('2 Confirmed product decisions')
table(s,['ID','Decision'],[
['DEC 01','DevWorks is one installed application that runs locally on macOS, Windows, and Linux with a modern, fast native UI and a small footprint.'],
['DEC 02','Rust is the working implementation direction. The product UI must not depend on a web UI or WebView. The final native toolkit remains open.'],
['DEC 03','The base installation must not require preinstalled .NET, Python, or Node. Optional compiler-backed language add-ins may require their own declared runtimes.'],
['DEC 04','The application rewrites the selected functionality of the existing applications; it must not require the old .NET hosts to remain running.'],
['DEC 05','Plain SQLite and sqlite-vec are selected for app storage. Application-owned node/edge tables supply graph persistence, SQLite FTS supplies lexical search, and sqlite-vec supplies vector search. HelixDB and GraphQLite are not app dependencies. Domain-focused Rust repository traits isolate storage implementation details and preserve replaceability.'],
['DEC 06','Tree-sitter supplies baseline parsing; optional SCIP-based semantic enrichment supplements it. Compiler-specific dependencies remain optional.'],
['DEC 07','A project may contain multiple repositories. Features may span several repositories within a project. SceneWorks with three managed repositories is a required example.'],
['DEC 08','The opening interface is a workspace coordinator chat. Existing project, feature, repository, intelligence, and review screens remain directly accessible.'],
['DEC 09','Global chat uses existing navigation on the left, the active conversation in the center, and chat session history on the right. A session dropdown is explicitly excluded.'],
['DEC 10','Feature work needs searchable, filterable, prioritized feature lists and a visible Kanban board of manually editable planning cards, with matching agent tools.'],
['DEC 11','Autonomous work remains observable and interruptible. Features and tasks retain their coordinator and worker conversations, Git actions, reviews, PRs, and execution history.'],
['DEC 12','All application inference goes through the router. Router configuration belongs in Settings; usage and costs have a separate screen.'],
['DEC 13','Memory, knowledge and lessons are consolidated in one Knowledge system and workspace, distinguished by tags. Dreaming, unresolved conflicts and notifications remain visible.'],
['DEC 14','Ordinary desktop use must not require a separate VM, OS account switching, a local dashboard login, or separately administered database and broker services.']
],[.75,6.15])
para(s,'The HTML prototype demonstrates layout and local sample interactions only. Its web presentation medium is not a decision to use web technology in the shipped application. Sample repository names, counts, costs, sessions, and decisions are illustrative rather than a live report of the user’s systems.')

s=section('3 Domain model and relationships','The data model must distinguish work identity, agent identity, conversations, and execution attempts. A conversation is evidence and a control surface; it is not the sole authoritative record of project state.')
table(s,['Entity','Responsibility and relationships'],[
['Workspace','Local application boundary containing projects, shared settings, provider configuration, workspace conversations, and cross-project oversight.'],
['Project','Managed product or initiative with goals, features, repository membership, scoped knowledge, activity, and coordinator visibility.'],
['Repository','Local checkout and remote identity, branches, worktrees, index state, changes, commits, checks, and pull or merge requests. Its Git state is independent of other member repositories.'],
['Feature','An outcome with requirements, acceptance criteria, priority, workflow state, repository scope, planning cards, coordinator lineage, and linked artifacts.'],
['Task or planning card','A story, task, bug or chore belonging to a project, optionally linked to a feature, with description, status, priority, acceptance criteria, dependencies, assignment and repository scope. Every task belongs to a project; its relationship to a feature is optional. A standalone task is shown as an issue and is directly executable and delegatable without creating a feature.'],
['Coordinator and worker','A logical agent identity and role. A feature coordinator owns execution coordination for its feature; workers have scoped assignments. A model choice is not an agent identity.'],
['Conversation and session','A user-facing conversation can persist across sessions and execution attempts. Sessions retain parentage, instructions, context, messages, tool calls, and routing records.'],
['Execution attempt','A particular running or completed attempt with state, ownership, leases, checkpoints, failures, budgets, and external effects.'],
['Artifact and event','A source reference, diff, commit, test result, review, PR, decision, instruction, or other evidence linked to work and the actors that produced it.'],
['Knowledge','One connected system for tagged memory, knowledge and lessons, with entities, claims, source evidence, confidence, scope, revisions and review relationships.'],
['Review and notification','A review item carries an unresolved decision and its consequences. A notification points to an event or item and has independent read state.']
],[1.35,5.55])
req(s,'MODEL-01','Confirmed','Project to repository membership must support multiple repositories. A feature must be able to coordinate work in more than one member repository without pretending there is one shared branch, commit, or PR.')
req(s,'MODEL-02','Proposed','All durable records must have stable IDs, scope, actor attribution, timestamps, and revision metadata where editable. Cross-links must survive agent restarts, feature pause, repository reindexing, and conversation changes.')
req(s,'MODEL-03','Proposed','Feature workflow, card workflow, and agent execution state must be separate. Pausing a coordinator must not move every card to a different workflow column or imply that acceptance has changed.')
para(s,'Projects support custom Kanban columns and status names. Backlog, Ready, In progress, In review and Done are a proposed initial template, not a fixed workflow. Proposed feature states additionally include Planning; execution states include Ready, Working, Pausing, Paused, Waiting, Failed, Cancelled, and Complete. The final state mapping remains open. Waiting on a dependency or decision must be distinguishable from a manual pause.')

s=section('4 Application architecture','The proposed architecture is a modular Rust application with an asynchronous execution runtime behind the native UI. It exposes a shared application command and query layer to both UI actions and agent tools. Logical components do not imply separately installed services.')
figure(s,'work/requirements-architecture.png','Figure 1 Proposed logical architecture inside one locally managed application')
table(s,['Component','Responsibility'],[
['Native presentation','Chat, work management, source control views, intelligence management, notifications, settings, and cost reporting.'],
['Application commands and queries','Typed validation, authorization, version checks, idempotency, actor attribution, work transitions, and event publication.'],
['Agent runtime','Workspace and feature coordinators, scoped workers, tool execution, session state, context assembly, supervision, and recovery.'],
['Inference router','Provider adapters, policy selection, capability checks, session affinity, limits, streaming, failover, and usage attribution.'],
['Code intelligence and knowledge','Tree-sitter indexing, optional semantic enrichment, graph traversal, retrieval, claims, lessons, rules, and dreaming.'],
['Execution and integration adapters','Git, process execution, local file access, optional language helpers, hosting-provider integrations, and future optional interoperable tool adapters.'],
['Persistence and scheduling','SQLite operational state, graph tables, FTS and sqlite-vec; durable queues, artifact files, recovery journals, and scheduled jobs behind storage traits.']
],[1.55,5.35])
req(s,'ARCH-01','Confirmed','The app must own the runtime for its selected capabilities. It must not require CodeGraph, dark-factory, simple-router, MariaDB, RabbitMQ, Docker, or a VM to be installed and administered as supporting servers. Optional container runtimes used by managed projects are separate from this core-app constraint under CONTAINER-01–03.')
req(s,'ARCH-02','Proposed','UI actions and agent tools must call the same validated Rust application operations. Neither may bypass work-transition rules, revision checks, attribution, or durable history by directly editing storage.')
req(s,'ARCH-03','Proposed','Long-running indexing, provider calls, process output, graph work, and background jobs must not block the UI event loop. Components must publish observable state changes that update open screens incrementally.')
req(s,'ARCH-04','Proposed','App-managed child processes are allowed for repository commands and optional helpers. A worker process using the same shipped application is a possible isolation mechanism, not a required microservice or account boundary. Final process topology remains open.')

s=section('5 Storage scheduling and consistency','Plain SQLite and sqlite-vec are selected. Internal graph data uses application-owned indexed node/edge tables; traversal and analysis use bounded SQL and Rust implementations behind domain contracts. Storage access is isolated behind Rust traits. Physical partitioning, migrations and publication protocols below remain design proposals; file and external effects must not be mistaken for database transactions.')
table(s,['Store','Proposed ownership'],[
['SQLite','Projects, features, cards, operational assignments and state, conversations and transcript indexes, commands and events, review workflow, notification state, queue leases, schedules, router settings and usage records.'],
['SQLite graph and search','Application-owned code/schema/Knowledge node and edge tables, evidence relationships, versioned index manifests, FTS lexical indexes and sqlite-vec vector indexes. No GraphQLite graph extension is selected.'],
['Local artifact files','Project artifact stores for screenshots, generated files, reports, attachments and large outputs shared by agents and humans through stable references. Git retains source history.'],
['OS credential store','Provider and Git-host credentials, or protected references to them. Plaintext secrets must not appear in transcript, queue, or routing diagnostics.']
],[1.2,5.7])
req(s,'STORE-01','Confirmed','The normal desktop configuration must use local embedded storage and persistent in-process scheduling rather than requiring a database daemon or message broker. SQLite must support durable queueing, not just transient in-memory notifications.')
req(s,'STORE-02','Proposed','A durable queue must support bounded claims, ownership, renewable leases, cancellation, priority, retry policy, terminal failures, and visible status. Restarts must recover pending work without treating every abandoned attempt as safe to replay.')
req(s,'STORE-03','Proposed','Authoritative domain changes, related history and durable queued intent must commit atomically through the application transaction contract. Derived graph, lexical and vector indexes may be prepared asynchronously, with operation identity, idempotent application, generation publication and visible freshness. A saved record must not imply its derived index is already current; database, file and external effects require explicit reconciliation where atomic commit is unavailable.')
req(s,'STORE-04','Confirmed','Before retrying an interrupted push, merge, database write or other state-changing tool operation, reconcile what actually happened. Record confirmed completion rather than duplicating it; retry only when the observed state makes that safe. If the coordinator cannot determine an operation outcome, pause the affected operation and dependent work, notify the user and let unrelated work continue. Provider recovery must account for partial output and uncertain request/cost outcomes under existing router policy. Queue deduplication does not prove exactly-once external execution. Reconciliation does not bypass existing manual-resume rules.')
req(s,'STORE-05','Proposed','Manual backup and restore under STORE-07 must cover the application database or databases and referenced artifacts at a consistent checkpoint. Define visible scope selection and backup/export controls. Code-derived indexes may be rebuilt; authored knowledge, conversations, instructions, decisions, and execution history must not be treated as disposable indexes.')
req(s,'STORE-06','Proposed','Schema migrations must be versioned and checked before write access. Storage failure, disk exhaustion, incompatible versions, and incomplete recovery must be visible, with preserved data and a diagnosable recovery path.')
req(s,'STORE-07','Confirmed','Backups of application databases and artifact files must be initiated manually. Do not run automatic backups.')
para(s,'SQLite plus sqlite-vec passed the recorded local comparison. GraphQLite cached BFS required explicit freshness management and did not improve the common graph workload; it is not selected. These are fixture-based Mac results, not full production, minimum-OS or Windows/Linux qualification. Historical candidate evidence is retained in docs/architecture; complete operation coverage, publication, migrations and target packaging still require validation.')

s=section('6 Code intelligence and language support')
req(s,'CODE-01','Confirmed','Tree-sitter must supply baseline structural parsing across project languages. Core code discovery must remain available without optional compiler-backed tooling. Grammar coverage must be explicit: unsupported grammars must yield an honest text-level fallback, not a false claim of semantic support.')
req(s,'CODE-02','Proposed','Baseline indexing must discover files, structural definitions, source spans, imports, and relationships that can be supported by the grammar and extractor. It must attach repository and revision provenance and identify inferred or unresolved relationships.')
req(s,'CODE-03','Confirmed','Optional semantic support must accept SCIP indexes and enrich the baseline with compiler-backed symbol and reference information. Language packs may also adapt native compiler index formats into the shared code graph, including Swift index stores; SCIP is not the only permitted input. SCIP is an interchange format, not a replacement parser or a guarantee that every language has an available indexer. All adapters must preserve source provenance and report supported and missing capabilities.')
req(s,'CODE-04','Confirmed','Provide optional compiler-backed semantic language packs for .NET, TypeScript, Swift and Rust. Each pack must declare its supported languages, project types, platforms, toolchain/runtime prerequisites and semantic capabilities. Missing or disabled packs must not prevent application startup or Tree-sitter baseline use. Pack installation does not replace the project toolchain or guarantee that every project can be indexed; unsupported configurations and partial results must be explicit.')
req(s,'CODE-05','Proposed','Indexing must support incremental updates, file deletion and rename reconciliation, cancellation, progress, failure reporting, and a manual rebuild. Semantic data must record its source revision and must not silently appear current after incompatible source changes.')
req(s,'CODE-06','Confirmed','CodeGraph-style discovery and context tools must be first-class agent capabilities: structural search, graph traversal, architecture and dependency inspection, source lookup, and contextual retrieval. Agents must not need to operate the old CodeGraph service to use them.')
req(s,'CODE-07','Proposed','The UI must expose repository index freshness, language coverage, semantic add-in state, source navigation, and the origin of relationships used in agent answers. Cross-repository relationships must preserve each repository’s own revision identity.')
req(s,'CODE-08','Proposed','Indexing and semantic helpers must have bounded concurrency, memory policies, deadlines, and process-tree cancellation. Deterministic failures must not drive endless retries or leave orphan compiler processes running.')

heading(s,'Branch and worktree overlays')
req(s,'CODE-09','Confirmed','Preserve CodeGraph branch-overlay functionality: query branch/commit changes over the repository baseline without modifying its default-branch index. Automatically include each agent’s uncommitted worktree changes in its graph view, isolated from main and every other worktree. Apply the view consistently to graph search, symbol/source lookup, traversal, dependency/impact inspection and context retrieval. Represent additions, modifications, deletions and renames. Retain repository, branch/commit and worktree provenance, visible freshness and coverage, and inspectable overlay lifecycle and failures.')
req(s,'CODE-10','Proposed','Identify overlay snapshots by repository, base/head revisions, worktree and local-content version; include staged, unstaged and indexable untracked changes. Select the agent’s own view automatically, with explicit scope for inspecting other views. Incrementally compose changed nodes/edges over unchanged baseline data, hide deleted or renamed-away data, and preserve relation confidence and unresolved links. Publish consistent snapshots atomically; baseline movement or concurrent edits must trigger reconciliation and visible stale/indexing state. Never silently answer a scoped query from main or another worktree, or present baseline-only analysis or stale semantic data as current overlay results.')
req(s,'CODE-11','Proposed','Expose overlay request/reuse, build/refresh, status, retry and retirement through UI and tools. Show branch/worktree scope and base/head/local-snapshot provenance in graph/source results and indexing logs. Preserve distinct queued, indexing, ready, failed, stale, expired and unavailable states; request reuse must not return incompatible snapshots. Retire derived overlays with owned worktree cleanup while retaining work history and evidence. Define active-overlay retention, expiry and rebuild policy separately from artifact retention. Missing or expired views must produce explicit unavailable/not-indexed results.')
heading(s,'Language boundary')
para(s,'The intent is broad language coverage. Tree-sitter remains the baseline and .NET, TypeScript, Swift and Rust are required optional semantic packs. Managed executable packaging is selected below; exact shipped grammars, extraction depth, supported toolchain versions, download distribution and platform coverage still require specification and validation. Building, testing or semantically indexing a repository may require its own toolchain; merely launching this application does not.')
heading(s,'Managed executable language packs')
req(s,'ADDON-01','Confirmed','Implement optional semantic add-ons as managed executable language packs that can be downloaded and installed without recompiling the main application. The desktop app discovers compatible packs and launches, monitors and stops their helper processes through a versioned extension protocol. No separately administered server, Docker container or VM is required. Harness-owned host and adapter code remains Rust; optional third-party compiler tooling may use its native runtime. Distribute prebuilt helpers for supported OS and CPU targets so installation does not require compiling a Rust crate locally. Cargo features and arbitrary downloaded crates are not the runtime extension mechanism.')
req(s,'ADDON-02','Confirmed','Provide a dedicated Add-ons management screen within Settings for available and installed language packs, including .NET, TypeScript, Swift and Rust. Users must be able to install, update, enable, disable and uninstall packs; inspect versions, language/project/platform support and prerequisites; check dependency and compatibility status; configure pack settings and project enablement; and navigate to indexing progress, errors and logs. Show effective availability separately from configured enablement, including not installed, disabled, missing dependency, incompatible, ready and failed states. Missing or failed packs must leave unrelated work and baseline indexing available.')
req(s,'ADDON-03','Proposed','Define a Rust SDK and a versioned manifest/protocol describing pack identity, publisher/source, version, host API compatibility, platform binaries, languages/project discovery, dependencies, configuration schema and indexing capabilities. Negotiate compatibility before execution. Use a local process transport, such as framed messages over standard input/output, with explicit request IDs, progress/diagnostic events and cancellation. Keep bulk index payloads separate from control messages where needed. Validate downloaded package identity/integrity and install atomically; distribution, publisher trust, update policy and rollback details remain open. New packs can extend declared host capabilities without rebuilding the app; new host extension points or arbitrary native UI behavior may require a host update.')
req(s,'ADDON-04','Proposed','Run semantic packs under app-managed scheduling and existing project/tool permissions. A separate process provides failure containment, not an OS security sandbox. Coordinate helper and compiler process trees with cancellation, resource limits, shutdown and restart recovery under CODE-08 and existing lifecycle rules. Attribute each run to its pack/version, repository, worktree, source snapshot and toolchain. Validate SCIP or native-adapter output before publishing a consistent graph update; reject incompatible or mismatched results and preserve the baseline on failure. Disable/update/uninstall must coordinate active runs and mark affected semantic data unavailable or stale without erasing work history. Define the exact active-run transition and uninstall retention mechanics before implementation.')
heading(s,'Semantic tooling candidates')
table(s,['Pack','Proposed semantic tooling','Optional prerequisites'],[
 ['.NET','Roslyn through scip-dotnet or a dedicated adapter; explicitly declare C#, Visual Basic and other language coverage.','Compatible .NET SDK and project dependencies.'],
 ['TypeScript','scip-typescript for TypeScript and JavaScript projects.','Compatible Node runtime and project dependencies.'],
 ['Swift','SourceKit-LSP and compiler index stores accessed through IndexStoreDB, mapped into the shared graph; no assumed existing Swift SCIP exporter.','Compatible Swift toolchain and project dependencies; Apple SDKs/Xcode for projects that require them.'],
 ['Rust','rust-analyzer SCIP generation, adapted into the shared graph.','Compatible Rust toolchain, Cargo project metadata and dependencies.']],[0.8,3.9,2.2])
para(s,'These are implementation candidates for the four confirmed packs, not completed integrations. Validate indexing coverage, native packaging, supported versions and failure behavior for each. Sources consulted 1 November 2026: https://github.com/sourcegraph/scip-dotnet ; https://github.com/sourcegraph/scip-typescript ; https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-analyzer/rust_analyzer/cli/index.html ; https://github.com/swiftlang/indexstore-db ; https://github.com/swiftlang/sourcekit-lsp/blob/main/Contributor%20Documentation/Background%20Indexing.md .')

s=section('7 Workspace coordinator behavior','The workspace coordinator is a distinct role with its own versioned instructions and tool access across the local workspace. It is the main point of contact when the user opens the app, not a chat box limited to the currently selected repository.')
req(s,'COORD-01','Confirmed','Opening the app must present a workspace coordinator conversation. The coordinator must be able to discover and inspect every project, feature, card, repository, active agent, relevant conversation, Git action, review, PR, and pending decision within the user’s accessible workspace.')
req(s,'COORD-02','Confirmed','The user must be able to ask for the status of any feature or PR, work across projects, active agents, and everything needing their input. Answers must be connected to the relevant work so the user can open the detailed screen directly.')
req(s,'COORD-03','Confirmed','The workspace coordinator must have tools to create, edit, prioritize, and control project work, and to start, continue, pause, inspect, or redirect the coordinators and workers doing it. Its scope must not be limited to summarization or read-only reporting.')
req(s,'COORD-04','Proposed','Before answering a consequential status question, it must read current authoritative records and relevant provider state. It must state freshness or uncertainty when unavailable and distinguish a planned step, a tool attempt, an observed result, and a verified outcome.')
req(s,'COORD-05','Proposed','The coordinator must preserve explicit human directions and explain material consequences of requested changes. It must link actions and status reports to stable work IDs, source evidence, sessions, and artifacts rather than leaving their only record in prose.')
req(s,'COORD-06','Proposed','Feature execution should be delegated to, or reconciled with, its attached feature coordinator. The workspace coordinator must not silently create a second competing execution owner when the user asks about work already running.')
req(s,'COORD-07','Proposed','Ambiguous references must be resolved from context where reliable, with clarification only when materially different actions remain possible. Routine reversible work within the user’s policy must not trigger repeated approval prompts.')
req(s,'COORD-08','Proposed','Workspace conversations must persist independently of feature conversations. New chat must preserve prior sessions and their work links. Starting another workspace chat must not duplicate projects, reset feature context, or launch new agents by itself.')
heading(s,'Proposed coordinator instruction contract')
para(s,'Role instructions must define purpose, scope, permitted operations, authority, delegation, evidence requirements, escalation, budget handling, and completion rules. Every session must record the instruction revision it used. Settings must let the user inspect and edit every agent role’s instructions under SET-03; running agents adopt instruction edits at their next safe step under SET-04, with repository instructions taking precedence over defaults under user directions and enforced permissions. The workspace coordinator’s broad tool visibility does not override user restrictions or scoped execution ownership.')
para(s,'Example conversations include “What needs my input across all projects?”, “How is the model import feature progressing?”, “Show the PRs for that feature”, and “Pause this feature and change the implementation direction”. The actual autonomous behavior is a product requirement; the current mockup only demonstrates sample responses and navigation.')

s=section('8 Feature coordinators and autonomous execution')
req(s,'AGENT-01','Confirmed','A feature must retain a relationship to every coordinator and worker that has worked on it. Selecting Start must launch an attached feature coordinator to review readiness under AGENT-12; selecting Continue on paused work must resume work with its prior history and current state available.')
req(s,'AGENT-02','Proposed','Start and Continue must be idempotent against already active work. Continuing must preserve logical coordinator identity and session lineage, even if the provider requires a fresh session or a different model. The app must reconcile cards, repositories, instructions, and external effects before new execution.')
req(s,'AGENT-03','Confirmed','A feature coordinator must plan and manage cards, delegate scoped assignments, use code intelligence directly, coordinate repository changes, monitor workers, arrange verification and reviews, and keep the feature’s status and history visible.')
req(s,'AGENT-04','Proposed','The execution loop must cover understanding requirements, discovering current code and knowledge, planning dependencies, implementing, validating, reviewing, reconciling findings, and recording acceptance evidence. Existing dark-factory strategies are references to evaluate, not assumed proven solutions.')
req(s,'AGENT-05','Proposed','Worker assignments must identify their originating issue or feature and cards, repository or worktree scope, acceptance criteria, instructions, context, limits, and ownership. Dependencies and resource constraints must be respected before conflicting work is dispatched.')
req(s,'AGENT-06','Confirmed','The intended mode is sustained autonomy with almost no routine human interaction. The app must keep working within configured authority and budgets, recover from ordinary failures, and ask for user input when an unresolved conflict or meaningful policy boundary requires it.')
req(s,'AGENT-07','Proposed','A coordinator must not mark work complete merely because an agent says it finished, a command returned success, a PR opened, or a child task stopped. Completion must be checked against the approved requirement and acceptance evidence; unresolved blockers must remain explicit.')
req(s,'AGENT-08','Proposed','Agent coordination must use durable assignments and renewable ownership rather than transcript-only conventions. Retries, stale plans, handovers, dependency changes, and concurrent manual edits must be reconciled with revision checks before work proceeds.')
req(s,'AGENT-09','Proposed','The scheduler must enforce independent per-feature and per-issue subagent limits and coordinate provider capacity, repository writes, compiler/indexing jobs, and other constrained operations. It must expose why work is queued or waiting and avoid dispatching work that cannot run. These operational constraints must not introduce a workspace-wide subagent-count cap.')
req(s,'AGENT-10','Confirmed','Settings must provide a configurable maximum number of concurrently active subagents per feature and per standalone issue, defaulting to 10 for each. Each feature and issue has its own independent allowance; there is no workspace-wide or global subagent-count cap. Fewer agents may be used when fewer useful tasks are ready.')
req(s,'AGENT-11','Confirmed','Feature coordinators and agents responsible for issues must maximize useful concurrency within the configured limit by running multiple independent, ready tasks at once and refilling capacity as tasks finish. Sequential execution must not be the default when parallel work is available. Dependencies and conflicting writes must be coordinated; a task waiting for user input must not stall independent work.')
req(s,'AGENT-12','Confirmed','Before feature implementation starts, its coordinator must assess execution readiness even when the user has already approved the plan. Review the intended outcome and scope, observable acceptance, implementation coverage, dependencies, access and resources, verification and delivery path, and material uncertainties across the whole feature. Every implementation detail need not be settled, but each material uncertainty needs a credible resolution path. On Start, develop incomplete planning from available evidence and seek required plan approval before implementing unapproved scope. Apply AGENT-18–20 to resolve gaps, justify blockers and reuse current assessments. An approved, ready plan must proceed without redundant planning or repeated approval requests.')
req(s,'AGENT-13','Confirmed','After feature-plan approval, the coordinator may adapt task breakdown, delegation, sequencing, scheduling and PR grouping autonomously to complete the feature quickly, efficiently and correctly. Approved requirements and configured concurrency, review and delivery policies still apply. A plan belongs to its feature. Keep the original plan active and approved while a revised plan awaits approval; continue eligible work under it. Approving the revision replaces the active plan; rejection leaves it unchanged. Pending changes cannot authorize new work. Use one active plan and one pending revision, without a user-facing plan-version system. Execution must remain visible and open to user refinement and redirection.')
req(s,'AGENT-14','Confirmed','The agent responsible for a standalone issue may delegate scoped work to subagents without creating a feature or manufacturing feature-owned task cards. An assignment may use the issue root and a scoped brief directly; optional linked task records belong to the same project and authorized execution scope. Preserve the parent/child agent relationships, assignments, transcripts, tool actions, changes, reviews and evidence on the issue. Delegated work follows the same router, tool permissions, worktree isolation, visibility and pause/redirect controls as other agent work. Issues use the configurable independent subagent allowance under AGENT-10, defaulting to 10, and maximize useful concurrency under AGENT-11.')
req(s,'AGENT-15','Confirmed','A clear, actionable standalone issue may proceed immediately to implementation without separate plan approval. The agent may plan and delegate as needed within the issue’s scope. Ask for input when requirements are unclear or an unresolved decision requires it. Existing tool permissions, user pauses, verification, review and Auto-Merge policies still apply; feature plan-approval requirements remain unchanged.')
req(s,'AGENT-16','Confirmed','Provide an optional per-project keep-working mode that automatically selects and starts the next highest-priority eligible feature or standalone issue through next_work(project). Make its configured state and activity visible and controllable in the project UI. Preserve plan readiness and approval, tool permissions, budgets, resource constraints and existing execution ownership. The mode must not resume explicitly paused work or bypass selective manual Continue after app closure. Run one feature or standalone issue at a time per project in this mode. Its subagents still use the independent per-item allowance under AGENT-10–11, and different projects may work concurrently. After reopening, keep-working requires manual restart. If no eligible work remains, stop until manually enabled again; later queue changes must not restart the mode automatically. Keep-working defaults to off for every new project; enabling it requires an explicit user action.')
req(s,'AGENT-17','Confirmed','Every newly started feature, standalone issue or task assignment must begin in a new session with clear context for that work, including when launched by keep-working mode, a scheduled agent or a user action. Assemble the relevant brief, instructions, project knowledge and scoped evidence; do not carry over the prior work item’s conversation as the new session’s transcript. Retain links to the originating action and relevant history without conflating session identities. Continuing an existing item preserves its established history and continuity; it is not a new work item.')
req(s,'AGENT-18','Confirmed','The coordinator must resolve what it reasonably can before escalating readiness gaps to the user: inspect current requirements, repository code, project knowledge and conventions; use available evidence and ordinary engineering judgment; and perform bounded investigation within existing authority and budgets. Record the decision and its confidence. A gap blocks affected implementation only when it materially threatens delivery or verification of an approved requirement and cannot reasonably be resolved through those means before that implementation proceeds. Routine reversible technical choices, stylistic preferences and requests for extra detail without a concrete consequence are not blockers. Escalate only remaining material choices or constraints requiring user input, authority or resources, with a specific question and recommended answer. Do not silently narrow approved scope, replace acceptance criteria or invent product intent to avoid escalation.')
req(s,'AGENT-19','Confirmed','Record readiness findings against the feature and relevant requirements as resolved decisions, bounded investigations, or matters needing a user decision. Each blocking finding must identify the missing or contradictory requirement/evidence, concrete delivery or verification consequence, affected work, what the coordinator checked or attempted, why autonomous resolution is insufficient, and the specific answer or evidence that closes it. Investigations must have a bounded objective, authority/resource limits, closure evidence and dependent-work gate; they do not authorize unapproved implementation. Keep unrelated eligible work moving. An undefined overall outcome may block the whole feature; a local gap must block only affected work and its dependents. Show the assessment, findings, resolutions and remaining questions through the shared work UI, agent tools and history.')
req(s,'AGENT-20','Confirmed','Readiness review has no finding quota; ready with no blockers is valid. Present remaining findings together after reasonable autonomous resolution, then check their closure without repeatedly reopening settled choices. Additional blockers require newly discovered material evidence or changed scope or assumptions. Reuse an assessment while its reviewed plan/scope and relevant evidence remain current; Continue alone does not require another full review. Material changes trigger reassessment of affected scope without invalidating unaffected approved work. Plan approval alone does not clear findings: record resolution or explicit, finding-specific human acceptance of an identified risk. Risk acceptance cannot supply missing prerequisites, make contradictory criteria achievable, waive enforced permissions or required verification, or authorize incomplete work to be reported complete. The feature coordinator remains accountable; a separate reviewer is not universally required.')
heading(s,'Agent role and model selection')
para(s,'Roles define responsibility and authority; the router chooses eligible provider/model configurations for requests. Coordinator, worker, reviewer, and dreaming roles may use different policies, but a model change must not create a new work identity or erase its conversation and evidence chain. Mandatory independent review is configurable; the remaining role catalog stays open.')

s=section('9 Visibility interruption and recovery')
req(s,'CONTROL-01','Confirmed','The user must be able to inspect any active work, its current step, assigned agents, transcripts, tool calls, process output, changes, and results, and interrupt to change direction. The app must not become a closed pipeline whose internal progress is inaccessible.')
req(s,'CONTROL-02','Confirmed','A human instruction must remain linked to the affected feature or assignment and its coordinator transcript. The UI must show that the instruction was sent, acknowledged, and incorporated, rather than silently treating typed text as an executed control action.')
req(s,'CONTROL-03','Proposed','Pause must stop new dispatch and request safe interruption of active work. Confirmed project archival is an exception under WORK-16: stop immediately without waiting for a safe step. The app must distinguish requested pause, acknowledged pause, and work that has not yet stopped. Cancellation must propagate to owned child processes and streams where supported; incomplete external effects must be reconciled.')
req(s,'CONTROL-04','Proposed','A redirect must invalidate or supersede incompatible queued work and context at a controlled revision boundary. Late agent results from an older plan must not overwrite newer human edits or be accepted as current completion evidence.')
req(s,'CONTROL-05','Proposed','After crash, quit, sleep, connectivity loss, or provider exhaustion, the app must retain conversations, assignments, queue state, checkpoints, outstanding decisions, and known external effects. Resume behavior must be explicit and must not assume an abandoned process completed or did nothing.')
req(s,'CONTROL-06','Proposed','Restart must reconcile leases and externally running processes or Git operations before resuming writes. Retries must be bounded and categorized by cause; repeated deterministic failures must stop or escalate with a useful explanation.')
req(s,'CONTROL-07','Proposed','A user must be able to inspect why work is paused, blocked, waiting on capacity, rate-limited, failed, or complete. Continue must not erase the condition that prevented progress or bypass an unresolved decision.')
heading(s,'Observable reasoning boundary')
para(s,'Visibility means recorded messages, user-facing plans and explanations, tool inputs and outputs, decisions, evidence, routing metadata, and execution state. It does not require access to a model provider’s private internal chain of thought. Provider-returned reasoning artifacts, where available and permitted, must not be confused with a complete or authoritative account of how a result was produced.')
heading(s,'Application shutdown')
req(s,'CONTROL-08','Confirmed','Closing the main application window must pause active agents and background jobs, persist their work and execution state, and exit the application. Work must not continue behind a menu-bar or tray icon after window close. Scheduled jobs do not execute while the app is exited. This explicit shutdown applies to all work; decision escalations remain task-scoped.')
req(s,'CONTROL-09','Proposed','Shutdown must stop new dispatch, request safe interruption, retain transcripts, checkpoints and queue state, and clean up owned child processes and provider streams with a bounded shutdown policy. In-flight external effects that cannot be confirmed must be marked for reconciliation rather than assumed undone or complete. Feature and issue work require manual resumption on reopening. Enabled maintenance/inference schedules reactivate on reopening, skipping missed occurrences. Interrupted maintenance/inference runs resume automatically; independent pauses, budget holds and approval waits remain. Feature and issue agents still require manual Continue.')

req(s,'CONTROL-10','Confirmed','After app restart, including a crash, feature and issue work that was active or whose activation was accepted but not launched must remain paused until the user explicitly Continues it. The UI must support selective resumption individually or together; unselected work stays paused. Preserve current instructions, history, unresolved decisions and dependencies, and resume only eligible work. Workspace coordinator chat remains available independently.')

req(s,'CONTROL-11','Confirmed','When the computer wakes from sleep while the app remains open, previously running feature and issue agents, maintenance jobs and scheduled inference runs must continue automatically. Sleep and wake alone must not introduce a manual-resume requirement. Work already paused or blocked must retain that state; automatic continuation does not clear a user pause or an unresolved decision. Manual feature and issue resumption applies after closing and reopening the app.')

req(s,'CONTROL-12','Confirmed','Paused work requires an explicit, authorized Continue. Start must leave it paused, including calls from the UI, agents and schedules. Agents and schedules may Continue human-paused work only with explicit user authorization; permission to start new work is insufficient. Existing manual-resume rules, permissions and unresolved holds remain effective.')

req(s,'CONTROL-13','Confirmed','Continue on a feature or standalone issue resumes its coordinator and all eligible descendant assignments. Child assignments have no manual pause control. Preserve child-specific blockers; continuing the parent must not clear them. Each descendant must pass its own eligibility checks before execution.')

s=section('10 Routing providers usage and costs')
req(s,'ROUTE-01','Confirmed','Every application inference request must go through one internal router path, including workspace and feature conversations, worker and review requests, scheduled inference tasks, planning, dreaming, context summarization/compaction, generated titles, semantic enrichment, embeddings, and accepted MCP-initiated inference requests. Agents and application subsystems must not bypass the router to call providers directly.')
req(s,'ROUTE-02','Confirmed','The router must choose suitable agents or models for the job through configurable policies. Its detailed configuration belongs in Settings and may remain hidden during ordinary work. Usage and cost reporting must be a dedicated screen.')
req(s,'ROUTE-03','Proposed','Selection must consider task or role policy, tool and modality support, context size, reasoning effort, availability, provider limits, cost preferences, and session affinity. A user must be able to inspect the selected route and material fallback reason for a request.')
req(s,'ROUTE-04','Proposed','Provider adapters must preserve streaming, tool-call identity and ordering, cancellation, errors, supported content types, and resumable conversation semantics. Retries must not duplicate exposed partial responses or repeat externally effective tool calls without reconciliation.')
req(s,'ROUTE-05','Proposed','Show provider waiting/failure reasons and the next retry time when known. Honor router-reported cooldowns under ROUTE-15 instead of repeatedly retrying cooling models. Missing authentication and indefinite unavailability need explicit recovery policy. Do not erase the run or route outside configured constraints.')
req(s,'ROUTE-06','Proposed','Usage must be attributable to project, feature, card or assignment where applicable, coordinator or worker, session, request, provider, model, and role. Router-selected catalog identity and any provider-resolved model identity must remain distinguishable.')
req(s,'ROUTE-07','Proposed','Reporting must distinguish provider-reported charges, estimated cost, unknown cost, token usage, and subscription capacity. Unknown price must not be represented as zero. A cost preference or routing tier must not be described as an enforced spending cap unless enforcement exists.')
req(s,'ROUTE-08','Confirmed','Each feature supports a spending limit or Off. A reached limit pauses further feature inference; Off tracks spending without a cap. Raising the limit or switching it Off must leave budget-paused work paused until the user manually selects Continue. Continue must still honor other pauses, blockers and current limits. Show attributed spend and effective budget state, preserving incurred usage and visible changes. Exact accounting and in-flight request handling remain open.')
req(s,'ROUTE-11','Confirmed','When a feature spending limit is configured, begin warning its coordinator at 75% of the budget so it can try to wrap up within the remaining allowance. Keep the warning and remaining-budget information available to the coordinator. Off has no budget-threshold warning. Budget pressure does not waive approved acceptance criteria, required verification or review, or permit incomplete work to be reported as complete.')
req(s,'ROUTE-12','Confirmed','Standalone issues do not support spending limits. Track and display their attributed inference usage and costs, without a per-issue cap, budget-threshold warning or budget-triggered pause. Feature budgets remain feature-scoped. Existing user pauses, approval waits, provider availability and selective manual resume after app closure still apply to issues.')
req(s,'ROUTE-13','Confirmed','Agents must be able to inspect screenshots and other images directly, including chat attachments and stored artifacts. Route image-dependent inference to a model and endpoint that support vision, through the same internal router. Do not silently omit images or claim visual inspection when no capable route is available.')
req(s,'ROUTE-14','Confirmed','Pin each session to its initially routed model. Reuse that pin across turns, tool continuations, compaction and app restarts. If rate-limited or unavailable, automatically switch the same session to an available eligible replacement and persist its pin, as in simple-router; retain it after the original recovers. Manual selection persists until changed or failover; Auto restores router selection with pinning. Replacements must meet required capabilities and configured routing constraints. Restoring a pin does not resume feature/issue execution: CONTROL-10 still requires manual Continue. Capability-change and other recovery mechanics remain open; cooldown waits follow ROUTE-15.')
req(s,'ROUTE-15','Confirmed','When no eligible model is available and the router reports cooldown expiry times, wait until the earliest eligible model cooldown ends, then automatically recheck availability and reconnect through the router. This temporary wait alone must not require manual Continue. If cooldowns change, honor the updated availability rather than repeatedly retrying early. Pin any replacement under ROUTE-14. Existing user pauses, approval waits, budget holds and app-restart manual-resume rules still apply. Recovery without a reported availability time remains open.')
req(s,'ROUTE-09','Confirmed','Support Codex, Claude subscription, Anthropic API-key, OpenRouter, and generic OpenAI-compatible connections, using simple-router as the subscription integration reference. OpenAI-compatible connections must accept configurable base URLs and model identities, including Ollama and LM Studio, rather than being limited to named products. Local and remote compatible endpoints must use the same internal router path as the subscription and API-key providers.')
req(s,'ROUTE-10','Confirmed','Use simple-router as the reference for provider connection and authentication behavior: Codex subscription device-code sign-in; optional direct Claude subscription browser/authorization-code sign-in at the user\'s risk, preserving simple-router behavior; a separate Anthropic API-key connection; OpenRouter provider-level API-key management; and optional API keys for configured OpenAI-compatible endpoints. Preserve connection status, token refresh and reauthentication, credential replacement/removal, model discovery/refresh, and connection testing where supported. Expose these controls in native Settings. Claude subscription is unsupported by Anthropic for third-party apps, disabled until explicit user risk acceptance, and distinct from API billing. Never silently exchange subscription and API credentials or billing. The Rust implementation must not require the old router server or external agent CLIs.')
heading(s,'Prompt construction and provider caching')
req(s,'CACHE-01','Confirmed','Provider prompt-cache efficiency is a critical harness requirement. Request construction, transcript handling, tool definitions and router behavior must avoid unnecessary harness-induced cache misses. Provider cache availability is not guaranteed; the requirement is to preserve eligible reuse and make its effectiveness observable.')
req(s,'CACHE-02','Proposed','Use a stable, versioned instruction and tool-definition prefix, with deterministic tool/schema ordering across built-in and MCP tools. Keep changing timestamps, progress, retrieved context and other live state out of the reusable prefix where protocol and instruction authority permit. Persist previously dispatched message/content boundaries and append new turns rather than rebuilding or extending old messages on each request. Meaningful instruction, skill and permission changes still take effect at their required safe step.')
req(s,'CACHE-03','Proposed','Preserve native assistant/tool-call/tool-result structure, stable call identities, content-block order and provider-required opaque continuation or reasoning blocks for same-provider continuation. Persist the ordering chosen for concurrent results. Do not flatten tool exchanges into prose, reformat prior results, or discard required continuation data during storage and replay. Cross-provider conversion must be explicit and protocol-correct; do not assume caches or opaque state transfer between providers.')
req(s,'CACHE-04','Proposed','Provider adapters must declare cache capabilities by actual model, endpoint and access mode, including supported enablement, breakpoint boundaries, keys, minimum lengths, retention and usage fields. Apply supported controls without silently sending incompatible options. OpenAI-compatible syntax or a subscription connection does not imply identical cache support. Preserve eligible session/model/upstream affinity within router policy, while allowing justified quality, capability, availability and policy-driven route changes. Record intentional changes that reduce reuse.')
req(s,'CACHE-05','Proposed','Compaction at the confirmed 80% threshold remains mandatory. Preserve stable instructions and reference blocks where possible, record the new compacted context revision, and build subsequent turns append-only from it. Cache optimization must not delay user redirection, retain unauthorized tools, bypass permission enforcement, suppress required fresh context or weaken task correctness. Evaluate total input cost and latency, including cache writes, rather than optimizing hit rate alone.')
req(s,'CACHE-06','Proposed','Usage and Costs must show provider-reported cached input, uncached input and cache-write tokens/costs where available, with attribution to requests and work. Distinguish unavailable metrics from zero and estimates from reported values. Provide bounded diagnostics for reusable-prefix changes, tool/instruction revisions, model/upstream changes, compaction and expiry where observable; inferred reasons must be labeled. Avoid duplicate sensitive prompt logging solely for diagnostics. Verify prefix stability and protocol replay across ordinary turns, tool calls, restart, retries, instruction/tool changes and compaction; use live cache metrics where the endpoint exposes them.')
heading(s,'Candidate functionality to retain from simple router')
para(s,'The source router includes provider authentication lifecycle, configurable model catalogs and tiers, context/capability eligibility, session affinity, local OpenAI-compatible endpoints, rate-limit failover, reasoning-effort translation, usage records, and separate monitoring/settings pages. These form the candidate retention surface. Provider families and authentication behavior are selected under ROUTE-09–10. Exact protocol/capability compatibility still requires validation; a compatible endpoint must not be assumed to support every tool, modality, or context size. The new app must not require the old router server to run.')
para(s,'A local inference endpoint may be optional. It is not a required new server and is not a commitment to bundle a local model runtime in the base application. Provider credentials and Git-host sign-in are distinct from the rejected requirement to switch OS users or sign into a separate local dashboard.')

s=section('11 Project features and planning cards')
req(s,'WORK-01','Confirmed','Project work must have full manual visibility and control. The feature list must support creation, editing, search, filters, prioritization, and access to in-progress, paused, ready, and completed work. Project membership and feature repository scope must be editable.')
req(s,'WORK-02','Confirmed','Each feature must have a visible Kanban board for planning cards. Humans and agents must be able to create, edit, reprioritize, assign and move tasks, and add or remove task-blocking dependencies through shared operations. This includes standalone issues and tasks in different features of the same project. Priority selects among eligible tasks; an unresolved prerequisite keeps a dependent task blocked regardless of priority. Accessible controls must accompany drag and drop.')
req(s,'WORK-03','Confirmed','All core project, feature, and card management operations exposed to the user must also be controllable through first-class agent tools, using the same domain rules. An agent action must appear in the same board and history as a manual action.')
req(s,'WORK-04','Proposed','A feature must hold requirements, acceptance criteria, description, priority, workflow state, repository scope, coordinator relationship, and related artifacts. A planning card must hold its own type, description, priority, workflow state, assignment, dependency links, acceptance criteria, and repository context.')
req(s,'WORK-05','Proposed','Dependency checks must prevent invalid cycles and identify blocked or stale prerequisites. A manually completed card must remain distinguishable from completion supported by verified acceptance evidence where those differ.')
req(s,'WORK-06','Proposed','Concurrent edits must use revision checks. A stale manual form or late agent update must be refused or explicitly reconciled while preserving the user’s draft. Silent last-write-wins behavior is unacceptable for instructions, planning state, and conflict resolutions.')
req(s,'WORK-07','Proposed','Ordinary archiving or removing work from a current view must preserve its history and links. Permanent project deletion is a separate explicit action under WORK-15. Repository removal from a project must not delete files, branches, transcripts, or evidence, and must explain active references before changing membership.')
req(s,'WORK-08','Confirmed','The user must always be able to refine a feature plan, both before execution and while agents are working, through direct management controls or coordinator conversation. Refinements must remain linked to the feature and its plan/history. The feature coordinator must review and incorporate changes into the current plan rather than treating the initial plan as permanently fixed.')
req(s,'WORK-09','Confirmed','Provide next_work(project, feature = null). Project is required. With feature omitted or null, select the highest-priority eligible feature or standalone issue from one project queue. Feature selection requires approval for implementation; skip incomplete or unapproved plans. Explicit Start may still initiate feature planning. Feature tasks are not separate project-queue candidates; with feature supplied, select the highest-priority eligible task within that feature and project. Scheduled agents may allocate/start work under these rules. Clear actionable standalone issues retain their existing no-separate-plan-approval policy.')
req(s,'WORK-10','Proposed','Allocate the highest-priority eligible work atomically with durable caller/session ownership; concurrent callers must not receive the same assignment. Use runtime request identities for idempotent retries, and expose selection/assignment, work kind/ID, version and no-eligible-work reasons. Validate that a feature belongs to the project. Respect dependencies, active ownership, explicit pauses, budget holds and approval waits. Define stable priority tie-breaking and lease/recovery semantics. Selection/claim and execution start are distinct; use the existing Start/coordinator path without duplicate coordinators or silently resuming user-paused features. Transfer ownership visibly to the feature coordinator or assigned worker and retain schedule/run provenance, context and Git/history links.')
req(s,'WORK-11','Confirmed','Every task belongs to a project and may optionally belong to a feature; standalone tasks are issues with the same durable identity. Humans and agents can create, manage and directly start tasks without requiring a feature, retaining their sessions, transcripts, Git/review, artifacts and activity history. Humans and agents may link and unlink issues. Linking makes resolution part of feature delivery and authorizes that addition without revised-plan approval. An existing agent notifies the feature coordinator and continues; otherwise the coordinator launches or resumes work when eligible. Existing pauses and blockers remain. Unlinking pauses work until manually resumed. Future spending uses the feature budget; earlier costs remain attributed where incurred. Keep existing branches/workspaces; the coordinator owns integration. Direct feature transfers continue under the new coordinator/budget while retaining holds.')
req(s,'WORK-12','Proposed','Provide project issue browsing/filtering, New issue with optional feature membership, and shared UI/chat/tool set/clear operations guarded by work revisions and project membership. Capture title, description/reproduction, expected/actual behavior where known, priority, status, acceptance criteria, repositories and source issue. Preserve one identity and its agent/session, transcript, Git/review, artifacts and history across association changes. Reconcile queues, supervisor responsibility, budget attribution and delivery routing without duplicate execution. Feature-linked work uses coordinator delivery; standalone work uses existing issue policies. Editable task details expose priority, blockers, Start/Pause/Continue, agents and history. Unlink uses a durable pause; relinking cannot clear it. Issue restart, concurrency and delegation retain CONTROL-10, AGENT-10–11 and AGENT-14.')
req(s,'WORK-13','Confirmed','Support custom Kanban columns and status names per project. Users must be able to manage their project workflow instead of being restricted to fixed global columns. Feature boards and project issues must use the applicable project statuses, with the same workflow available to agents through first-class tools.')
req(s,'WORK-14','Proposed','Give project statuses stable identities and explicit workflow semantics for allocation, readiness, review and completion; agents and next_work must not infer behavior solely from editable display names. Provide column creation, renaming and ordering with equivalent tool operations. Removing an occupied status requires an explicit destination for affected work, preserving history. Keep workflow status separate from execution pause/wait state; moving a card must not bypass plan approval, checks, reviews or merge policy. Refine the exact status mapping and transition rules before implementation.')
req(s,'WORK-15','Confirmed','Removing a project from active management must archive its records and history by default. Preserve its work records, linked agent sessions, transcripts, decisions, Git/review history and evidence under the existing retention rules. Permanent deletion must be a separate explicit action, never a side effect of archiving or ordinary project removal. Provide the same project-management behavior through the UI and permitted first-class tools under WORK-03.')
req(s,'WORK-16','Confirmed','If any agents are actively working on a project when archival is requested, warn the user and ask whether they are sure they want to archive it before proceeding. Declining leaves the project and its work unchanged. On confirmation, stop all project work immediately: block new dispatch and interrupt active coordinators, subagents, inference/tool operations, owned processes and background work without waiting for a safe step, task completion or a cooperative checkpoint. Archived projects must not start new work through keep-working, project schedules or other dispatch paths. Retain recorded history and interrupted-work state; unrelated projects continue. This applies to archival initiated through the UI or agent tools. Restoring an archived project must leave its work, keep-working mode and project schedules stopped until the user explicitly reactivates them. Restoration alone must not resume execution.')
req(s,'WORK-17','Confirmed','Permanent project deletion must remove only its harness data and artifacts. Leave local repositories, including their files, worktrees and branches, and all GitHub resources untouched. Deletion must not trigger repository cleanup or remote GitHub mutations. Apply the same boundary to the UI and permitted agent tools; ordinary archival remains nondestructive under WORK-15. Preserve shared artifacts while another project still references them. Shared-data ownership and deletion mechanics remain to be specified.')
heading(s,'Manual and autonomous control')
para(s,'The app must support both conversation-driven management and direct manipulation. The user must not have to ask an agent to perform an ordinary board edit, and an agent must not have to drive UI widgets to update a card. Manual changes made during execution must be visible to the relevant coordinator and reconciled with its active plan.')
para(s,'The board in the prototype associates each card with a primary repository while the feature spans repositories. Whether a single card can itself affect multiple repositories remains open. The final solution must not force cross-repository work to lose its relationship or fabricate an atomic multi-repository commit.')

s=section('12 Source control and complete work history')
req(s,'HISTORY-01','Confirmed','From a feature, task, or related project work, the user must be able to browse the full linked history of coordinators and workers, transcripts, tool actions, Git actions, reviews, merge requests or PRs, decisions, and outcomes.')
req(s,'HISTORY-02','Proposed','History must be a durable queryable relationship graph and event record, not a concatenated log alone. Events must identify actor, work scope, operation, timestamp, relevant repository and revision, and linked evidence. Corrections and reversals must preserve prior records.')
req(s,'GIT-01','Confirmed','Source control overview must preserve repository boundaries inside a project and feature. Branches, worktrees, changed files, diffs, commits, PRs, checks, reviews, and merge state must be attributed to their actual repository.')
req(s,'GIT-02','Confirmed','All agent coding and integration work must use Git worktrees. Each concurrent coding task must have its own isolated worktree for each repository it changes; tasks must not share a writable working tree or edit the primary checkout. The feature coordinator integrates task changes using a worktree. Worktree ownership must remain linked to the task, agent, history and feature where applicable. Preserve existing user changes without requiring separate OS accounts or a VM.')
req(s,'GIT-03','Confirmed','Source-control views must support inspection of repository and branch state, changes/diffs, commits, worktrees, PRs, checks and review findings, with links to the producing agent session. Built-in manual staging, committing, branch management and merging are not required; the user uses an external Git client or GitHub for those operations. This does not remove the harness’s configured autonomous Git and delivery capabilities.')
req(s,'GIT-04','Proposed','Automated commits, pushes, PR creation or updates, reviews, conflict resolution, and merges must follow configured authority and branch policies. A multi-repository feature must track its integration dependencies and each repository’s result without claiming an atomic merge across repositories.')
req(s,'GIT-05','Proposed','Reviews and verification evidence must identify the source revision they assess. New changes must invalidate or refresh relevant conclusions. Successful tool completion must not be reported as passing checks, review approval, or merge success without observed evidence.')
req(s,'GIT-06','Confirmed','When a feature completes, automatically remove all harness-created worktrees and local branches associated with that feature across every involved repository, including task and integration worktrees/branches. Retain transcripts, commit identities, PR/review links, and the historical ownership records after the local Git resources are removed.')
req(s,'GIT-07','Proposed','Before cleanup, verify resource ownership and integration evidence and stop any remaining writers. Cleanup must not discard uncommitted work or unintegrated commits, or remove unrelated user resources. Record each result; expose failures and a retry path instead of silently reporting successful cleanup. Paused features retain the worktrees needed for continuation.')
req(s,'GIT-08','Confirmed','Features must always use a feature branch in each affected repository. Task PRs target that feature branch. The feature coordinator chooses whether related tasks share a PR or have separate PRs; one PR per task is not required. Open the final feature PR to main only once the feature implementation is complete, never as an early task-delivery path. The final feature PRs remain linked to the same feature and its task/group PR history.')
req(s,'GIT-09','Proposed','Distinguish implementation completion, final PR delivery, and cleanup in execution state and the UI. Implementation completion permits the final feature PR to main; review/fix/check and Auto-Merge policies still govern its merge. Keep needed worktrees and branches while final delivery is pending. Run feature-completion cleanup after final integration is verified across the affected repositories, and show cleanup failures explicitly. This sequencing preserves the required branch while its final PR is open.')
req(s,'GIT-10','Confirmed','Every task must document its associated current worktrees, branches and PRs so the user can easily find where its work lives. Show the repository, worktree path, branch name and PR link on the task, including each relevant repository for cross-repository work. Grouped-task PRs must be linked from every covered task. Preserve historical associations after cleanup and distinguish removed or closed resources from current ones.')
req(s,'GIT-11','Confirmed','Standalone issues use an isolated task branch and worktree and open PRs directly to main. They do not require a feature branch. Preserve configured review/fix/check and Auto-Merge policy and show branch/worktree/PR links on the issue. Tasks belonging to features retain the existing feature-branch delivery flow.')
req(s,'GIT-12','Confirmed','After verified merge of an issue to main, automatically clean up its harness-owned worktrees and local branches. Retain transcripts, artifacts, GitHub links and complete work history. Preserve unintegrated work and show cleanup failure/retry state under GIT-07.')
req(s,'GIT-13','Confirmed','Agents may automatically force-push their own task branches, including after a rebase. Force-pushing feature branches requires explicit user approval; branches outside an agent-owned task branch must not receive automatic force-push authority. Never force-push to main. This prohibition applies regardless of Auto-Merge settings, package/tool configuration or an approval for another operation, and must cover equivalent forced updates to the main ref. Existing repository access controls and branch protections remain in effect; approval does not authorize bypassing them. Record permitted force-pushes, approvals, denials and outcomes in the linked task/feature and agent history.')
req(s,'GIT-14','Proposed','Before a force-push, resolve the actual repository, remote and destination ref, check task-branch ownership and effective branch protection, and validate any required approval against that concrete operation. Use an explicit expected remote revision with force-with-lease or an equivalent guarded update so concurrent remote changes cause refusal and reconciliation rather than overwrite. Unknown ownership must not qualify for automatic authority. Enforce the main prohibition for harness-dispatched Git operations, including forced refspecs and equivalent invocation forms. Define preflight, shell/tool enforcement and approval-binding mechanics, preserving the absolute main prohibition under GIT-13.')
req(s,'GIT-15','Confirmed','For a feature spanning multiple repositories, all final feature PRs must pass their required checks and reviews before any final PR is merged. Once every final PR is ready, merge them in dependency order. Apply each repository’s effective Auto-Merge, review and approval policies; readiness does not grant authority to merge when that authority is disabled or awaiting approval. Track each repository’s delivery result and the feature’s overall delivery history.')
req(s,'GIT-16','Confirmed','Resolve routine merge conflicts autonomously and continue delivery without notifying the user or requesting routine conflict-resolution approval. Concurrent agents working in the same repository are expected to cause conflicts; the coordinator must reconcile their changes while preserving the approved feature requirements. Conflict resolution does not waive required checks, reviews, repository merge authority or the prohibition on force-pushing main. If a merge fails in an unusual way that the coordinator cannot resolve, pause the affected delivery and dependent work and notify the user with the failure, attempts and evidence. Independent work continues; ordinary recoverable conflicts are recorded in history without a user notification.')
req(s,'GIT-17','Confirmed','A feature is complete once all of its final feature PRs are verified as merged into main across the affected repositories. Track releases and deployments separately, retaining links to the feature, repositories, revisions and delivery history. A pending, approval-waiting or failed release or deployment must not keep the merged feature open. Release and deployment actions retain their separate configured authority under DELIVERY-09. Feature completion triggers the required cleanup of harness-owned worktrees and local branches under GIT-06–09; retain the feature’s history and artifacts and expose cleanup outcomes separately.')
heading(s,'Completion and integration policy')
para(s,'The user wants almost fully autonomous delivery, but the exact default authority for pushing, opening PRs, merging, release actions, destructive Git operations, and protected branches has not been agreed. The app must make this policy explicit before executing those actions. It must allow sensible trusted autonomy without creating a mandatory approval prompt for every ordinary action.')
para(s,'History retention must include failures, retries, interrupted sessions, superseded plans, previous coordinators, and abandoned branches where they explain the work. History search and filters must support at least actor, event type, work identity, repository, and time or session context.')

heading(s,'Project artifact store')
req(s,'ART-01','Confirmed','Every project must have a local artifact store for screenshots, files, reports and other work outputs. Agents must be able to save, discover and read artifacts through first-class tools and share stable references with other agents and humans. Humans must be able to add and inspect files through the UI. Artifacts must retain explicit links to their originating project and, where applicable, feature and task, with navigation from that work to the artifact and back. Later reuse must preserve those origin links.')
req(s,'ART-02','Proposed','Give each artifact stable identity and metadata for name, media type, size, tags, creation time, producing agent/session, feature/task and relevant repository revision. Preserve source relationships and versions instead of silently overwriting referenced evidence. Show attachments in conversations, task/feature history and a searchable project Artifacts view, with image/text previews and file open/export controls.')
req(s,'ART-03','Proposed','Store artifact contents as local files managed outside disposable worktrees, SQLite metadata and graph provenance links. Publish complete files atomically and preserve references across restart, agent handoff and Git cleanup. Feature completion must not delete its artifacts when branches and worktrees are removed. No separate object-storage server or cloud account is required.')
req(s,'ART-04','Proposed','Artifact access must honor project scope and effective tool policy, including read-only reviewers. Supply bounded previews or file references to agents rather than inserting every file into model context. Define storage location, quotas and deletion behavior in Settings; retain artifacts until explicitly deleted under ART-05 and show disk/write failures. Include contents and metadata in backup/restore and avoid silently deleting referenced evidence.')

req(s,'ART-05','Confirmed','Keep project artifacts until explicitly deleted. Automatic expiration or cleanup must be disabled by default; artifact age, feature completion, worktree cleanup and storage pressure must not silently remove retained files. Storage limits must produce visible status rather than implicit deletion.')

s=section('13 Primary chat interface','The workspace conversation is the face of the product. Project management, source control, routing, and knowledge remain available around it, without turning the opening screen into a metrics dashboard.')
figure(s,'outputs/ui-08/01-chat-current.png','Figure 2 Approved Chat layout with Current and Chat History tabs integrated header and floating completion cards')
req(s,'UI-01','Confirmed','The main chat screen must place existing navigation on the left, the active workspace coordinator conversation in the center, and chat session histories in a right-hand sidebar. It must not use a conversation dropdown in place of that sidebar.')
req(s,'UI-02','Confirmed','The center conversation must support a persistent composer, readable messages and tool or result summaries, and links to the work being discussed. The initial state must clearly invite a conversation with workspace-wide scope. All chat composers support image/file attachments under UI-08 and a model selector under UI-10.')
req(s,'UI-03','Proposed','The right sidebar must list prior workspace conversations, show the active selection, support search and useful date grouping, and provide New chat. Switching sessions must restore the correct transcript and preserve drafts. Feature coordinator conversations remain attached to their features.')
req(s,'UI-04','Confirmed','The user must be able to leave chat to inspect a detailed screen and return without losing the conversation, its session selection, or its connection to ongoing work.')
req(s,'UI-05','Proposed','Coordinator role instructions, workspace scope, and relevant context must be inspectable. Detailed routing configuration must remain in Settings, while request attribution and cost may be inspected when useful.')

s=section('14 Detailed UI workspaces','The reviewed project, feature, repository, review and notification workspaces remain the baseline. Memory, knowledge and lessons are consolidated into one tagged Knowledge workspace. Chat remains the primary entry point alongside direct management screens.')
figure(s,'outputs/ui-08/04-feature-board.png','Figure 3 Approved feature board with integrated header repository scope and its attached coordinator conversation')
table(s,['Surface','Required purpose'],[
['All projects','Oversee projects and active or paused work, then open a project or feature directly.'],
['Project overview','Search, filter, prioritize, create, and edit features; inspect member repositories, active coordination, decisions, and recent activity.'],
['Feature workspace','Kanban board, requirements and acceptance, code changes by repository, history, context, and a persistent feature coordinator conversation. Start, Continue, Pause, and redirect controls remain connected to execution state.'],
['Project tasks','Create, search, filter, prioritize and inspect tasks, including standalone issues with no feature. Task details expose execution controls, agent conversation/history, evidence and current Git locations.'],
['Repositories','Membership, local paths and remotes, branch and worktree state, changes, PRs, and indexing or semantic coverage.'],
['Agents and activity','Inspect coordinators, workers, sessions, assignment state, current work, errors, and attributed project history.'],
['Knowledge','One searchable workspace for memory, knowledge and lessons, distinguished by tags; shared inspection/editing, evidence, confidence, scope, freshness, revisions and learning lifecycle controls.'],
['Artifacts','Project files and screenshots shared by humans and agents; browse, search, preview, attach and inspect provenance.'],
['Dreaming','Consolidation runs, instruction versions, budgets, reports, journals and unresolved conflicts; link changes back to the unified Knowledge workspace.'],
['Review and notifications','Actionable conflicts with evidence and affected work, and a global notification inbox with deep links and independent read state.'],
['Settings and usage','Router and provider setup, policies, optional add-ins, appearance and notification preferences; separate usage and cost exploration.']
],[1.35,5.55])
req(s,'UI-06','Proposed','Every long-running operation must expose current state, progress where meaningful, cancellation or pause where supported, and recoverable errors. Empty, loading, offline, partial-data, failed, and stale-data states must be designed rather than represented as fabricated success or zero values.')
req(s,'UI-07','Proposed','Keyboard navigation, readable focus states, screen-reader labels, text scaling, accessible status changes, and large histories must be supported. Resize behavior must preserve the conversation and essential controls. Theme and pane-sizing options remain open.')
req(s,'UI-08','Confirmed','Every chat input must accept images and files through a selection dialog, drag-and-drop and paste. Apply this to workspace, feature, issue and every other interactive agent chat composer. Attachments must accompany the correct message and remain accessible from its conversation history; image inspection follows ROUTE-13.')
req(s,'UI-09','Proposed','Show pending attachments with filenames and image previews where supported; allow removal before sending and preserve them with per-chat drafts. Store durable copies and stable attachment references, retaining conversation and applicable project/feature/task provenance through the artifact system. Workspace attachments without a project retain their conversation scope until associated with work. Define file formats, size limits, clipboard handling and extraction; show unsupported or failed processing rather than pretending unread content was analyzed. Preserve original attachments through context compaction and provider changes.')
req(s,'UI-10','Confirmed','Every chat input must include a model selector populated from models available in the router. Selections persist for that chat until changed or replaced by failover under ROUTE-14. An Auto option returns selection to the router; its resolved model is session-pinned. All requests still pass through the router and respect permissions and required capabilities.')
para(s,'The revision 08 screenshots illustrate the approved layout, navigation and sample interactions under UI-14/15. Exact native color values, typography metrics and pane widths remain implementation details within that visual direction. Prototype screenshots do not substitute for native acceptance.')

s=section('15 Context memory knowledge lessons and rules')
para(s,'Memory, knowledge and lessons are one connected Knowledge system, differentiated by tags in a single workspace. Their useful metadata remains: claims retain evidence and confidence; decisions retain rationale; lessons retain applicability and learning history. CodeGraph already models Lesson as a KnowledgeKind with additional learning lifecycle state. The rewrite preserves needed behavior without inheriting separate catalogs. Project/shared scope is independent of category tags.')
req(s,'KNOW-01','Confirmed','The user must have one Knowledge workspace consolidating memory, knowledge and lessons, differentiated by tags. It must support inspection, tag search and filtering, manual creation and editing, lifecycle changes, and source evidence. Agents must use the same connected knowledge system through tools.')
req(s,'KNOW-02','Proposed','Memory must represent entities and atomic claims with predicates, values, scope, evidence, author or agent provenance, confidence, timestamps, and revision history. Conflicting observations must remain inspectable until resolved; confidence must not be presented as proof.')
req(s,'KNOW-03','Proposed','Knowledge must support goals, decisions, constraints, facts, gotchas, attempts, architecture notes, and lessons, with active, superseded, or retracted lifecycle states as appropriate. Verification must record the evidence and source revision used, and later source changes must be able to mark entries stale. Each decision records who made it. A decision the user made or approved, including one approved in chat and recorded by an agent, names the user as its origin; only a decision an agent made on its own is marked autonomous. Autonomous decisions stay visible for reading and never ask the user to acknowledge them; the review queue under REVIEW-01 holds only conflicts an agent cannot resolve.')
req(s,'KNOW-04','Confirmed','Lessons and rules must be visible with their origin, applicability, recurrence, challenges, and relationship to the work where they were learned or used. Agents must be able to retrieve and manage them through tools.')
req(s,'KNOW-05','Confirmed','The lesson lifecycle must distinguish candidate evidence, proposed rules, effective rules, and rejected proposals. Human instructions, review-derived lessons, and repeated corrections must retain their distinct provenance; recurrence alone must not be treated as proof of effectiveness. A rule’s origin decides how it takes effect. A rule from the user’s own instruction or review, given directly or relayed by an agent with the user’s words attached, takes effect immediately without approval; it is the user’s own edit under EVAL-05 and is monitored under EVAL-07. A lesson inferred by an agent, subagent or automated reviewer stays candidate evidence until it recurs across a configured number of distinct source events, then takes effect automatically without approval and is monitored under EVAL-07 like any other change that takes effect without approval under EVAL-05. No rule asks the user for approval; the user may promote a candidate early, or challenge or retire any rule, at any time. Taking effect is not proof that a rule works. Eval evidence under EVAL-08 may show whether a lesson rule is effective.')
req(s,'KNOW-06','Proposed','Rule delivery must be inspectable: the app must record which relevant rules and revisions were supplied to a session. It must expose whether a lesson has been addressed in code, instructions, a skill, documentation, or a test, and surface unmatched or unlearned evidence where useful.')
req(s,'KNOW-07','Confirmed','General lessons must be available across projects automatically without manual sharing or promotion for each project. Project-specific knowledge and conventions must remain scoped to their project. Shared lessons must retain their origin and applicability; automatic availability does not make every lesson relevant to every task. Project-scoped content must remain scoped in agent retrieval and context assembly, including when dreaming consolidates it.')
req(s,'KNOW-08','Confirmed','Consolidate memory, knowledge and lessons into one Knowledge system and workspace, differentiated by tags rather than independent catalogs. Preserve identity, evidence, relationships, revisions and applicable learning metadata without duplicating the same item across categories. Scope remains a separate property: a lesson tag alone must not share project-specific content. Human and agent tools must support the same tagging and scoped retrieval operations.')
req(s,'KNOW-09','Confirmed','Knowledge search must let an agent bound one page of results by a token budget, filled with the best-ranked claims first. The budget sets the page size and must not make any claim unreachable: the remaining claims stay available on later pages. The default budget and the largest budget a caller may pass must be configurable, inherited Workspace then Project; a caller may pass its own budget on a call within the allowed range. A person searching in the app is not limited by the budget.')
req(s,'CONTEXT-01','Proposed','Context assembly must combine the work brief, current plan, instructions, relevant code and graph results, scoped knowledge, applicable lessons, prior evidence, and handover state within a deliberate token budget. It must preserve provenance instead of indiscriminately replaying all repository content and chat history.')
req(s,'CONTEXT-02','Proposed','A session must record its instruction revisions, retrieved context sources, important omissions or truncation, and routing metadata sufficiently to explain what information the agent acted on. Context updates and summaries must preserve unresolved constraints and decisions.')
req(s,'CONTEXT-03','Proposed','Resolve enforced permissions and explicit user directions before repository guidance. Applicable repository instructions override default role instructions. Resolve repository conflicts by nearest directory first, only within its subtree; at one directory AGENTS.md overrides CLAUDE.md, whose nonconflicting instructions still apply. Preserve nonconflicting ancestor rules. Discover only authorized instruction roots, retain exact source digests/applicability in each effective context and reconcile material unresolved conflicts visibly. Same-level ambiguity cannot grant authority or silently affect another repository.')
req(s,'CONTEXT-04','Confirmed','When an agent reaches 80% of the active model’s maximum context, the harness must automatically compact its working context and continue without user intervention. Preserve the complete original transcript and the same logical agent identity and work relationships. Compaction must not replace or truncate the stored transcript; record the compaction and resulting continuation in the agent’s session history. Any compaction inference must use the router.')
req(s,'CONTEXT-05','Proposed','Measure context against the actual routed model/endpoint limit, including instructions, tool schemas, messages and tool results with appropriate modality accounting. Reserve response/tool headroom and check request size before dispatch, including after model changes. Compaction must preserve current requirements, plan state, user directions, unresolved decisions and relevant evidence/artifact references. Unknown limits, oversized single inputs and failed compaction need visible bounded recovery rather than silent truncation or loss of work.')
req(s,'CONTEXT-06','Confirmed','Automatically load applicable repository and subdirectory AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md instructions. Instructions closest to the target files override conflicting ancestor instructions within that subtree. At the same directory level AGENTS.md wins conflicts; CLAUDE.md adds nonconflicting guidance. Repository instructions override default role instructions. User directions and enforced permissions remain above all file instructions. Preserve path applicability across multiple directories/repositories; files cannot grant tool authority.')
para(s,'Exact knowledge import/export, entity merge/unmerge, retention, classification of general versus project-specific content, and rule-promotion policy need capability-level review. General lessons are shared automatically; project-specific knowledge and conventions remain project-scoped under KNOW-07. These are not blanket exclusions: the selected CodeGraph core must be assessed and every required capability represented before implementation scope is approved.')

s=section('16 Dreaming review queue and notifications')
req(s,'DREAM-01','Confirmed','Dreaming must consolidate and maintain memory, knowledge, and lessons with visible runs and outcomes. The user must be able to inspect what was considered, what changed, what was proposed, and what remains unresolved.')
req(s,'DREAM-02','Proposed','Dreaming must have inspectable base and project instructions with revision history, manual and scheduled triggers, budgets, cancellation, and bounded execution. Every run must record the instruction revisions, source scope, usage, actual outcome, and errors or unfinished work.')
req(s,'DREAM-03','Proposed','Dreaming must verify against current evidence, preserve provenance and human corrections, and separate direct permitted changes from proposals requiring review. Dispatch success must not be reported as successful consolidation. Partial and budget-exhausted runs must remain distinguishable from completed runs.')
req(s,'DREAM-04','Proposed','Direct changes must have an action journal with before and after revisions and a guarded revert path. A revert must not overwrite subsequent human or agent edits; conflicts must be reported and proposals must remain separately identifiable.')
req(s,'DREAM-05','Confirmed','Dreaming must automatically apply consolidations when there is no conflict, without requesting approval for each change. Preserve the visibility and project-scoping requirements. Conflicts an automated agent cannot resolve must enter the review queue and notify the user; independent non-conflicting consolidation can continue.')
req(s,'REVIEW-01','Confirmed','The app must provide an approval or review queue for conflicts an automated agent cannot resolve. Items must show the competing records or proposed change, evidence, origin, affected project and work, and the decision required from the user.')
req(s,'REVIEW-02','Proposed','The queue must distinguish a human-escalated decision from a proposal that an authorized agent can review. Agents must not impersonate a human resolution. Resolution must retain actor, rationale, revisions, and resulting changes, with expected-version checks against the records being resolved.')
req(s,'REVIEW-03','Confirmed','An escalation must pause only the task needing user input and work dependent on that task or merge. Agents must continue unblocked tasks in the feature. The entire feature should wait only when that merge blocks the remaining feature work. Resolving one item must not clear unrelated conflicts; existing instructions remain effective while a proposal is pending.')
req(s,'NOTIFY-01','Confirmed','In-app notifications must surface required input and other meaningful work events, with direct links to their project, feature, session, review, or report. Notifications must be available from the main chat and detailed workspaces.')
req(s,'NOTIFY-02','Proposed','The global inbox must support read and unread state, category filtering, and duplicate suppression. Reading or dismissing a notification must not resolve its linked review item. Resolution and acknowledgment are separate operations.')
req(s,'NOTIFY-03','Proposed','Notification policy should cover decisions, failures, blocked work, significant completions, review changes, budget or authentication problems, and dreaming reports. Severity, quiet preferences, aggregation and OS alerts for other categories remain open; required-input OS alerts follow NOTIFY-04.')
req(s,'NOTIFY-04','Confirmed','When the app is not focused, items needing user input must also trigger native operating-system notifications where supported and permitted. Respect OS authorization and notification suppression. Keep the in-app notification and approval item available if OS alerts are denied, unavailable or suppressed. Displaying, reading or dismissing an OS notification must not resolve the decision. Native integration and per-platform behavior require validation.')

s=section('17 Platform quality privacy and operations')
req(s,'PLAT-01','Confirmed','Minimum OS targets are macOS 26.2 and Windows 10. Linux is also required, with supported distributions and versions to be determined during architecture validation from what the chosen components can support. Do not claim every Linux distribution is supported. Core installation and launch must not require preinstalled .NET, Node or Python. The same primary Rust codebase must provide the selected behavior; CPU architectures, packaging formats and the validated compatibility matrix remain to specify.')
req(s,'PLAT-02','Confirmed','The application must work without an app server or separately administered storage infrastructure. Local browsing, planning edits, and history inspection must remain useful without provider connectivity. Provider-dependent actions must show their actual waiting or failure state.')
req(s,'PLAT-03','Proposed','Dependency claims must distinguish the app runtime from optional semantic helpers, Git, repository build toolchains, and optional local inference. The app must detect missing tools and state exactly which operation needs them; optional components must not become accidental startup requirements.')
req(s,'PERF-01','Confirmed','The app must feel fast, modern, and lightweight. Startup, idle memory and CPU, installed size, active indexing, graph queries, transcript scrolling, and multiple concurrent agents must be measured separately rather than summarized by a single unverified footprint claim.')
req(s,'PERF-02','Proposed','UI lists and transcripts must scale through incremental updates and bounded rendering. Indexing, vector caches, process output retention, provider concurrency, and background schedules must have visible resource controls. Numeric targets and benchmark datasets remain open.')
req(s,'SEC-01','Proposed','Use one normal desktop user context. Store secrets through appropriate OS facilities, redact them from ordinary logs, and show what project content is sent to external providers. A separate local web login and OS user switching must not be part of ordinary use.')
req(s,'SEC-02','Proposed','Autonomous filesystem, shell, network, Git, and integration access must be governed by explicit project scope and user policy, with attribution and interruptibility. A Git worktree provides write isolation, not a security sandbox. Any stronger optional isolation must not recreate mandatory VM infrastructure.')
req(s,'SEC-03','Confirmed','Make agent access and trust restrictions configurable, supporting broad/full-access use as well as users who prefer tighter restrictions. Full access is the initial default for new projects; tighter access remains selectable. Show effective permissions and access mode in Settings. Existing role restrictions, including always-read-only code reviewers, explicit approvals and the prohibition on force-pushing main remain effective; full access does not waive them. Configuration scope and OS-enforced isolation options require architecture validation without mandatory VM infrastructure.')
req(s,'SEC-04','Confirmed','Provide basic safety checks on tool calls to prevent accidental deletion of entire drives or repositories. Keep these checks active even for full-access agents. Check destructive operations before dispatch, expose blocked operations and their rationale, and preserve attribution in the relevant work history. These are additional guards, not replacements for configured tool permissions. Drive/filesystem roots remain blocked. Deliberate deletion of a registered primary repository requires a dedicated user-confirmed action; agents cannot bypass this with operation approval or full access.')
req(s,'SEC-05','Proposed','Centralize safety preflight for built-in and brokered shell/process, MCP and package tool calls. Resolve actual deletion targets, including path roots, parent traversal, wildcards and links/reparse points; protect filesystem/volume roots and registered repository roots. Avoid relying only on literal command-name or string matching. Distinguish bounded cleanup of owned task worktrees from deletion of a primary repository. Define enforcement for opaque tools and native executable add-ons honestly: harness policy checks alone do not provide OS confinement against arbitrary code running with the user’s permissions. Validate any stronger claimed restriction at the process/OS boundary. Rule coverage, bypass resistance and platform-specific enforcement remain architecture validation work.')

req(s,'OPS-01','Proposed','The app must provide local diagnostics for storage, queue health, orphan processes, indexing, provider failures, and recovery. Data export, backup/restore, schema migration, and update behavior must preserve authored records and explain failures.')
req(s,'OPS-02','Proposed','The app must not require a central product account or remote control plane for normal operation. Repository hosting and provider integrations may use their own credentials. Provider connections follow ROUTE-09–10; Git-host authentication methods still need selection and documentation.')
para(s,'Native toolkit selection must be proven against the actual interaction surface: long streaming transcripts, rich text and code, large editable boards, accessible controls, diff rendering, pane resizing, clipboard, shortcuts, and packaging across the three platforms. egui/eframe is a candidate and is used in the inspected PhotoCraft application; Slint and Iced were also discussed. No toolkit is declared selected here.')

heading(s,'Agent UI testing and automation')
req(s,'UIQA-01','Confirmed','Provide first-class agent tools for testing websites through browser control and for testing native desktop applications through computer control. UI tests must exercise the application under development and expose their activity and results in the harness. Browser control does not change the native egui UI decision.')
req(s,'UIQA-02','Proposed','Use browser DOM/accessibility targets and native accessibility controls where available, with screenshots and pointer/keyboard input when needed. Native automation needs platform adapters and explicit permission/capability status on macOS, Windows and Linux. Unsupported controls or missing permissions must produce visible limitations rather than false test success.')
req(s,'UIQA-03','Proposed','Link UI test sessions, actions, screenshots, assertions, failures and relevant browser/process logs to the feature, task, responsible agent and tested repository revision. The user must be able to inspect, interrupt and redirect testing. Record observed outcomes; completing an input action alone does not prove a workflow passed.')
req(s,'UIQA-04','Proposed','Isolate concurrent browser sessions and test data. Coordinate access to shared desktop focus, mouse and keyboard through the resource reservation system under RES-01–03 so agents cannot interfere with each other. This resource constraint must not become a global subagent-count cap. If the user is active, desktop takeover requires OK or Wait under UIQA-05; idle detection and approval lifetime remain open. Mutating UI tests must run under an authorized testing/implementation role, preserving always-read-only reviewers.')
req(s,'UIQA-05','Confirmed','Before taking control of a desktop that is not idle, ask the user and offer OK or Wait. OK authorizes the pending takeover; Wait defers it and must not be treated as approval. Do not inject input or steal focus while awaiting the answer or after Wait. Show the request and waiting state in the app. Independent feature work must continue. Idle detection and retry mechanics remain to be specified.')
req(s,'RES-01','Confirmed','Provide a resource reservation system modeled on CodeGraph resources and holds, exposed through first-class tools and visible in the UI. Support named resources with capacity, acquire, renew and release operations, resource/holder/waiter inspection, purpose, priority, expiry and ownership linked to the feature, task and agent session. Reservations coordinate finite resources such as desktop input, test environments, ports, databases and constrained hardware; they are distinct from per-feature and per-issue subagent limits.')
req(s,'RES-02','Proposed','Use a canonical resource identity across projects for physically shared resources, including one machine desktop. Scope project-private resources separately. Enforce ownership and capacity at harness-controlled operation dispatch for resources such as desktop input; CodeGraph advisory holds alone are insufficient to prevent concurrent input. Queue unavailable resources by priority and FIFO within a priority; unrelated tasks continue.')
req(s,'RES-03','Proposed','Persist reservation state and use atomic allocation, idempotent retries and explicit renewal. Track grant, release, expiry, cancellation, queue position and unreachable ownership. An expired lease does not prove its process stopped: stop or reconcile old writers and reject stale owners before transferring a physical resource. Show recovery and reservation history in the UI, including pause, shutdown and sleep/wake reconciliation.')
para(s,'Backend selection remains open. Playwright is a browser-automation candidate, not a chosen dependency; browser binaries and any driver/helper runtime need a provisioning plan consistent with the Rust rewrite and no preinstalled Node/Python/.NET requirement. Framework test harnesses can complement full desktop testing; egui_kittest offers Rust UI interactions and snapshots for the harness itself and compatible egui targets. Native computer control is still needed for broader application and OS integration testing.')

s=section('18 Open decisions before implementation','These questions are part of the requirements review. They must be resolved, explicitly bounded, or represented as tracked work before an implementation plan is considered complete.')
table(s,['ID','Decision to settle'],[
['OPEN 01','Final native UI toolkit and supported OS versions and CPU architectures. Establish measurable app footprint and responsiveness targets on representative projects.'],
['OPEN 02','Exact CodeGraph core capability inventory and exclusions. Assess import/export, knowledge merge/unmerge, diagnostics, documentation/wiki, conventions, analysis jobs, and any additional graph tooling without assuming blanket parity.'],
['OPEN 03','Exact dark-factory and simple-router capabilities to preserve. Separate useful behavior from infrastructure and unproven coordination strategies.'],
['OPEN 04','Initial provider and Git-host adapters; subscription/OAuth versus API-key authentication; optional local endpoints; token and cost accounting semantics.'],
['OPEN 05','Default autonomy authority for pushes, PRs, review, merge, releases, destructive actions, protected branches, and escalation. Define policies without requiring approval for every routine action.'],
['OPEN 06','Final state machines, configurable workflow columns, card types, repository scope for individual cards, scheduling priority, concurrency, and execution ownership rules.'],
['OPEN 07','Agent-role instructions are editable in Settings; repository and applicable subdirectory instruction files load automatically, supplement roles and cannot override user directions or tool permissions. Agents compact automatically at 80% of the active model context limit, retaining full transcripts and identity. Set token accounting and recovery, remaining instruction-resolver implementation details and file-update mechanics, remaining role definitions, precise safe steps, rule promotion, knowledge classification and how human corrections reach agents. General lessons are shared automatically; project-specific knowledge and conventions remain scoped.'],
['OPEN 08','SQLite and sqlite-vec packaging on all targets; physical data partitioning, versioned schema, transaction/snapshot contracts, index publication, resource limits and backend-neutral repository conformance. The store choice is settled; alternate engines are not an implementation prerequisite. Validate graph workloads, vector indexing, scoped filtering, footprint and recovery against the selected SQLite adapter contracts. Vector embeddings and embedding the database engine in-process are separate capabilities.'],
['OPEN 09','Always use task and integration worktrees, with feature-completion cleanup. Task/group PRs target feature branches; final PRs to main follow feature implementation completion. The coordinator groups tasks. Manual Git operations use an external Git client or GitHub; tasks show current worktrees, branches and PRs. Set Git integration details, base refs/naming, cleanup recovery and unusual cross-repository failure recovery mechanics. Routine merge conflicts are resolved automatically without notifying the user; unusual failures the coordinator cannot resolve pause affected delivery and dependent work and notify the user under GIT-16. All final feature PRs must pass required checks and reviews before any merge, then merge in dependency order under GIT-15. Standalone issue PRs target main and trigger automatic local cleanup after verified merge. Feature completion follows verified merge of every final feature PR under GIT-17; track releases and deployments separately.'],
['OPEN 10','Window-close and background operation, tray/menu-bar behavior, restart resumption policy, sleep handling, and control granularity beyond individual features.'],
['OPEN 11','Branch overlays and isolated automatic uncommitted worktree views are confirmed. Refine snapshot/rebuild/retention mechanics, baseline movement and semantic coverage. Set shipped Tree-sitter grammars, extractor depth, optional semantic indexers/dependencies, helper distribution and indexing freshness policy.'],
['OPEN 12','Existing data import and migration scope, retention, transcript/artifact storage, backup/export formats, and whether external MCP or tracker integrations are part of the initial product.'],
['OPEN 13','Notification severity and OS integration, themes, panel resizing, session organization, app updates, and initial setup workflow.']
],[.8,6.1])
para(s,'Confidence is high in the confirmed product direction and reviewed layout. Confidence is medium in the proposed component boundaries and operational policies until validated. Performance, packaging, recovery correctness, and complete source capability parity have not been demonstrated by the prototype.')

s=section('19 End to end acceptance scenarios','These scenarios define observable outcomes for the eventual implementation. They are review criteria, not claims that the prototype implements the system. Detailed tests must be derived from the approved requirements and risk of each slice.')
table(s,['Scenario','Expected result and requirement coverage'],[
['First launch','A base install opens workspace chat on each target OS. Navigation is left, active conversation center, searchable session history right, with no conversation dropdown or mandatory framework/runtime setup. DEC 01–09; UI 01–05; PLAT 01–03.'],
['Multi repository project','A project containing three repositories supports a feature spanning two of them. Each repository keeps its own revisions, worktrees, checks, and PRs. MODEL 01–03; WORK 01–07; GIT 01–05.'],
['Manual and agent board control','A human creates and prioritizes a feature, creates and moves cards, and an agent performs the equivalent operations. Both paths enforce the same rules and show attributed history; a stale edit preserves the draft. ARCH 02; WORK 01–07.'],
['Start and Continue','Start creates/selects the coordinator and reviews the existing plan. A ready authorized plan proceeds; incomplete planning produces a candidate plan and approval/input request before unapproved work. Pause is acknowledged; Start on paused work through the UI, an agent or a schedule leaves it paused and requests explicit Continue. Agents and schedules cannot Continue human-paused work without explicit user authorization; allowing new starts is insufficient. Authorized Continue preserves current state and lineage without duplicate ownership, lost artifacts or bypassing unresolved holds. Continue feature/issue resumes its coordinator and eligible descendants, including nested assignments, while child-specific blockers remain effective; manual child pauses are not supported. AGENT 01–09, 12; CONTROL 01–07, 12–13.'],
['Cross project question','The workspace coordinator answers a question about a feature, PR, active agents, or required input using current records, with links and explicit uncertainty where applicable. COORD 01–08.'],
['Interrupt and redirect','The user inspects a worker transcript and tool activity, changes direction, and sees acknowledgment and plan reconciliation. Late old-plan results do not overwrite current instructions. CONTROL 01–07; CONTEXT 01–03.'],
['History exploration','Opening a completed or paused feature reveals its coordinator lineage, workers, transcript sessions, Git changes, reviews, PRs, verification evidence, decisions, and failures. HISTORY 01–02; GIT 01–05.'],
['Language fallback','A repository is indexed structurally without optional runtimes. Installing a compatible semantic add-in enriches it; disabling or failing that add-in leaves honest baseline capability and freshness state. CODE 01–08.'],
['Provider and budget failure','An exhausted or failing provider creates a visible wait or bounded fallback under policy. All inference remains routed; request attribution, reported/estimated/unknown costs, and partial response recovery remain correct. ROUTE 01–08.'],
['Optional feature budget','Off tracks spend without a cap. Configure a limit: coordinator warnings begin at 75%, with remaining-budget information; at the limit, further inference pauses. Raise the limit or switch it Off: work stays paused until manual Continue, which still honors other blockers and limits. Verify shared coordinator/worker/review attribution and concurrent calls without invented prices for unknown-cost routes. Retain incurred usage; required checks and acceptance still govern completion. ROUTE 06–08, 11; AGENT 07, 13.'],
['Unified Knowledge','Create memory, knowledge and lesson items in one workspace; tag, search, filter and edit them manually and through agent tools. Verify shared identity/history and retained evidence and lesson metadata, without duplicate catalogs. Changing category tags must not change project/shared scope. KNOW 01–08.'],
['Knowledge scope','Record a general lesson in one project and verify automatic availability to another project with its origin and applicability retained. Record project-specific knowledge and a convention; verify that another project does not receive them through retrieval, context assembly or dreaming consolidation. KNOW 04, 07; CONTEXT 01; CONV 01–02.'],
['Knowledge conflict','Contradictory evidence creates a review item without erasing either source. Only affected work waits. A reasoned resolution checks current revisions and is linked to the work; reading a notification alone changes no decision. KNOW 01–06; REVIEW 01–03; NOTIFY 01–03.'],
['Dreaming and revert','A run automatically applies non-conflicting consolidation without per-change approval, preserves scope and records instructions, usage, changes and outcome. Unresolved conflicts enter the review queue and notify the user while independent consolidation continues. A later edit causes a revert conflict rather than an overwrite. DREAM 01–05; KNOW 07; REVIEW 01; NOTIFY 01.'],
['Crash and restore','Interrupt a push, merge, database mutation and tool operation before execution and after an effect but before local acknowledgment. Reconcile outcomes before retry; never repeat completed effects. Unresolved outcomes hold affected/dependent work and notify the user; unrelated work continues. Crash after activation commit but before launch: pending feature/issue work stays paused after reopening until selective explicit Continue. Preserve other holds. Trigger manual backup/restore; verify no automatic backups run. STORE 01–07; CONTROL 05–07, 10; OPS 01–02.'],
['Desktop usability','Long transcripts, large backlogs, indexing, concurrent agents, and resized panes meet agreed targets without a blocked UI or hidden resource runaway. Accessibility and offline management remain usable. UI 06–07; PERF 01–02; SEC 01–02.']
],[1.3,5.6])
para(s,'Before build approval, all open decisions that affect architecture or an initial vertical slice must be closed or explicitly tracked. The native application must be verified against these requirements, not against whether its screens merely resemble the HTML mockups.')

